  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1242 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nSimilar to 24 hours ago, mostly cloudy skies have kept\ntemperatures running a few degrees cooler this morning. Ongoing\nconvection in southeast Kansas continues to stretch a thick\ncirrus shield across the region, limiting heating early this\nmorning. A few cloud breaks are expected, but heating today\nshould remain tamed with high temperatures ranging from the upper\n70s to mid 80s, which will aid in RH values remaining at or above\ncritical thresholds. Broad upper ridging will persist into the\nNorthern Plains through midweek keeping dry and comfortable\nweather in place. Southerly flow will take over and advect warmer\nand moister air beginning Tuesday as temperatures creep towards\nor exceed 90 degrees. Humidity values and temperatures are\nexpected to remain elevated from Tuesday through the rest of the\nweek.\n\nA cutoff low will linger and weaken over Texas next week, with\nenhanced moisture from Polo to work with, but the details are\nmurky especially when it comes to how long it lingers. The GFS\nmay be a bit too progressive, and there seems to be a better\nconsensus for a bit slower progression, like the ECMWF and EC-\nAIFS suggest. All of this is to say that the last few days of the\nforecast period are uncertain. The uncertainty greatly affects\nour temperature and precipitation forecast, but while the system \nremains to our west, expect warmer temperatures and increasing\nrain chances through the end of the week. Uncertainty in the\nforecast remains elevated in the long range noted by 10-15 degree\ntemperature differences between the NBM 25th and 75th percentiles\nlate next weekend into early next week. Be sure to keep an eye on\nthe forecast as we monitor the evolution of the incoming system.\n\nDNM",
